{{define "content"}}
<h1>Accept your invitation</h1>
<p class="sub">Enter the address this invitation was sent to, your name, and a password. The address is asked for deliberately: it is what stops a forwarded invitation from working for whoever it was forwarded to.</p>
{{with .Problem}}<p class="problem">{{.}}</p>{{end}}
{{- /* The second of the two forms that opt IN to autofill (invariant 23).
       The justification: this is where somebody's account comes into existence,
       so the address is their own and the password is one they are choosing
       now -- "username" and "new-password" are exactly true here, and a manager
       offering to generate and store the password is the outcome we want.
       Contrast the invite form, where the address belongs to somebody else. */ -}}
<div class="card" style="max-width:30rem">
  <form method="post" action="/invite">
    <input type="hidden" name="token" value="{{.Token}}">
    <label for="email">Email address</label>
    <input id="email" type="email" name="email" value="{{.Email}}" autocomplete="username" required autofocus>
    <label for="name">Your name</label>
    <input id="name" type="text" name="name" value="{{.Name}}" autocomplete="name" required>
    <label for="password">Password</label>
    <input id="password" type="password" name="password" autocomplete="new-password" required>
    <label for="confirm">Password again</label>
    <input id="confirm" type="password" name="confirm" autocomplete="new-password" required>
    <p><button class="primary" type="submit">Create my account</button></p>
  </form>
</div>
<p class="muted">Accepting an invitation does not sign you in: it creates the account and sends you to the sign-in form, where the password you just chose gets used straight away.</p>
{{end}}
